3 #uuid "eae0e3e4-3d5e-500f-a238-5ead6dabaad9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410540 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410540 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410540 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f02020d2-3fed-5ef7-a4e4-c92e5db8fab5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410541 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410541 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410541 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410541 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "620ffecc-6446-571f-9e30-35668717eb9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bc8cd6e0-fec4-59f6-bf63-f95c9e73406a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410543 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410543 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410543 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410543 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410543 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410543 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410543 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410543 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410543 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6ce72d9-3525-5097-88ee-c623de804b20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410544 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410544 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410544 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410544 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f77cbe95-6bf7-5129-86d2-ca625369224e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w})=2$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a087057-8a12-50fd-9bce-b13921276b0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w})=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3$ (not $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410546 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a1e15f70-9949-53c2-8d3b-1dd646d018c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410546 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]})=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410547 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410547 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410547 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410547 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a83939de-1a77-573b-b113-0b677761ad51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410547 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]})=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410548 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5153a05-4fb4-55bc-add5-e73b5a837403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410548 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
